.\" splitlen(1) -- man-macro reference page
.\" $Revision: 1.3 $
.\" $Log: splitlen.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:35:44  dlr
.\" Correct the output-template placeholder and show the $HEX[] length effect. The placeholder is # not %; splitlen.c sets OutLenId to hash at line 412 and the page had it wrong throughout, including both examples, so the documented commands produced plain.txt_NN rather than the intended split. Also records what happens with no placeholder at all: the suffix lands on the whole name including extension, giving plain.txt_16 and not plain_16.txt. -u now shows why it matters, $HEX[636166c3a9] measuring 16 bytes as written against 5 decoded, and warns that a rehex-processed wordlist otherwise collapses into one or two long-length buckets. All three behaviours verified by running.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:16:00  dlr
.\" Remove the redundant .PP immediately after .SH EXAMPLES. mandoc reports it as "skipping paragraph macro: PP after SH"; groff does not flag it at all, which is how it survived a groff-only check. macOS and the BSDs render with mandoc, so mandoc is what most readers of these pages actually see. No rendered output changes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: splitlen(1) man page. All 5 options; documents the -o template placeholder, append-not-truncate behaviour, and that -u changes what length is measured.
.\"
.TH SPLITLEN 1 "2026-08-29" "splitlen 1.3" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
splitlen \- split a file into separate files by line length
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B splitlen
.BI \-o " name"
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR char ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B splitlen
reads the named
.IR file s,
or standard input if none are given, and writes each line to an output file
chosen by that line's length.  Output files are opened and closed on demand,
so any number of distinct lengths is handled without running out of
descriptors, and any line length is supported.
.PP
This matters for masked and length-restricted attacks: a wordlist split by
length lets each length be run against exactly the rules, masks and hash
types that can produce it, instead of running everything against
everything.
.PP
.B \-o
is required.  Its argument is a template.  If the template contains the
placeholder character \(em
.BR # ,
unless changed with
.B \-c
\(em the placeholder is replaced with
.BI _ length\fR,
so
.B out#.txt
yields
.BR out_5.txt ,
.B out_6.txt
and so on.
.PP
If the template contains no placeholder the same string is appended to the
whole name, extension included:
.B \-o plain.txt
produces
.BR plain.txt_16 ,
not
.BR plain_16.txt .
Put a
.B #
where you want the length.
.PP
An existing output file is appended to, not truncated, so a split may be
built up over several runs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " name"
Output filename template.  Required.
.TP
.BI \-c " char"
Change the placeholder character in the
.B \-o
template.
.TP
.B \-u
Decode
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
wrappers on input before measuring length.  Without this, a hex-wrapped
line is measured and split by the length of its
.I encoding,
not of the bytes it represents.  The difference is large:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$HEX[636166c3a9]   ->  16 bytes as written
                   ->   5 bytes decoded
.fi
.RE
.IP
A wordlist that has been through
.BR rehex (1)
will therefore land almost entirely in one or two long-length buckets unless
.B \-u
is given.
.TP
.B \-h, \-?
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage error, a missing
.BR \-o ,
or an allocation failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
Split a wordlist into
.BR words_5.txt ,
.BR words_6.txt
and so on:
.PP
.RS
.nf
splitlen \-o words#.txt wordlist.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same, measuring the decoded length of hex-wrapped lines:
.PP
.RS
.nf
splitlen \-u \-o words#.txt wordlist.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR rling (1),
.BR dedupe (1),
.BR rehex (1),
.BR mdxfind (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
